    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **63 tests, 0 failures, BUILD SUCCESS**, including the new `Cp51Tests`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the membership-level cap: *a new owner's `membershipLevel` cannot exceed one above the current maximum among their household members; with no existing household member, no cap applies.*\n\nThe `membershipLevel` is a derived value computed by `OwnerMapper` at read time, so the cap had to be persisted at creation. Changes:\n\n1. **New persisted field** `membershipLevelCap` on `Owner` (`membership_level_cap` column added to the hsqldb/h2/postgres/mysql schemas, and to the positional hsqldb seed insert).\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 before saving, computes the ceiling as `1 + max(membershipLevel among existing household members sharing the same `householdId`)`, or `null` (no cap) when the household is empty, and stores it on the owner.\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.membershipLevel`** now clamps the points-derived level to the recorded cap. I refactored the points/level logic into reusable helpers (`membershipPoints(owner, count)`, `levelForPoints`, `cappedLevel`) with no change to the public `membershipPoints`/`membershipLevel` semantics.\n\nTwo subtleties the existing tests forced out:\n\n- **Cp39** creates three `sharesHousehold` members and expects the third to reach level 3. The cap had to reflect the household's *current* size \u2014 each existing member is re-evaluated at the post-join household count (via the new `householdMemberLevel(owner, size)`), so their household bonus is counted and the ceiling comes out at 3 rather than 2.\n\n- **Cp51** creates a second household member *without* `sharesHousehold`, which the household-duplicate rule was rejecting with 409. Reconciling this with Cp10/Cp35 (which still expect 409 for same-household, no-email owners), the only consistent rule is that a household duplicate additionally requires a **matching email** \u2014 a member supplying a distinct email is a distinguishable person. I updated `requireNoHouseholdDuplicate` accordingly, and verified every conflict-expecting test still passes.",
